<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
    §9.8.1 — four durations and three easings. Nothing animates in this application
    without a row in §9.8.2, which is a short table; if a moment is not in it, the
    answer is that it does not animate.

    WzDurationInstant is 0 ms and is not a placeholder. §9.8.2 gives it to readout value
    changes, the medallion ring redraw, and severity state changes — the things a bench
    user reads at a glance from across a room. An animated readout turns "the number
    changed" into "something is moving", and on an instrument that is a lie. Naming zero
    as a token rather than leaving those cases untokenised is what makes the decision
    visible to the next person.

    None of the tokens in this file is referenced anywhere yet, and a reference count
    run over Themes/ on 15 Aug 2026 (#125) will report all seven as unused. They are
    not dead, and deleting them would be wrong twice over.

    Most of §9.8.2's rows describe animations that do not exist: there is no card-enter
    stagger, no ConnectedAnimation between the windows (OverviewPage.xaml records why),
    and no reorder animation on the satellite table. The one animation the application
    does have, the Details page transition, is a SlideNavigationTransitionInfo - and
    that type exposes neither a duration nor an easing, so WzDurationNormal and
    WzEaseStandard cannot reach the row §9.8.2 assigns them to. That is part of #120,
    which is filed against §9.8.2 for naming two APIs that do not exist.

    So these tokens are the specification's motion vocabulary held ready, in a file
    whose whole argument is that naming a decision makes it reviewable. Removing them
    would leave the next animation to invent its own numbers, which is the thing the
    file exists to prevent.
-->
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Durations                                                     -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <!-- Readout value changes, medallion ring redraw, severity state changes. -->
    <Duration x:Key="WzDurationInstant">0:0:0</Duration>

    <!-- Hover, pressed, checked, focus visual. -->
    <Duration x:Key="WzDurationFast">0:0:0.15</Duration>

    <!-- Page transition, expand/collapse, InfoBar in and out, flyout. -->
    <Duration x:Key="WzDurationNormal">0:0:0.25</Duration>

    <!-- Window first-run entrance, and nothing else. -->
    <Duration x:Key="WzDurationSlow">0:0:0.4</Duration>

    <!--
        §9.8.2 card enter: 30 ms stagger, at most four cards.

        Milliseconds as a Double rather than a TimeSpan because WinUI's x: namespace has
        no x:TimeSpan — that is a WPF intrinsic, and asking for it is a XAML compiler
        error rather than anything the C# compiler would catch. Code that needs a
        TimeSpan builds one with TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds; the four Durations above are
        already the right type for everything declared in markup.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="WzStaggerIntervalMilliseconds">30</x:Double>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Easings                                                       -->
    <!--                                                               -->
    <!-- KeySpline control points from §9.8.1. Declared as             -->
    <!-- KeySpline resources so a storyboard references the token      -->
    <!-- rather than retyping four numbers, which is how easing        -->
    <!-- curves drift apart across a codebase.                         -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <!-- Two-way transitions, expand and collapse. -->
    <KeySpline x:Key="WzEaseStandard" ControlPoint1="0.8,0" ControlPoint2="0.2,1" />

    <!-- Entrances. -->
    <KeySpline x:Key="WzEaseDecelerate" ControlPoint1="0.1,0.9" ControlPoint2="0.2,1" />

    <!-- Exits. -->
    <KeySpline x:Key="WzEaseAccelerate" ControlPoint1="0.7,0" ControlPoint2="1,0.5" />

</ResourceDictionary>
